# Messages of lib/checks/10-system.sh. Format and rules: see common.lang.

section.10-system=System

# ---- SYS-OS ----
SYS-OS.other.title=Not an Ubuntu system ({name})
SYS-OS.other.impact=This tool is written for Ubuntu. Most checks still work on systems based on Debian, but support dates and some package names may be wrong for yours.
SYS-OS.other.act.1=Shows the name and version of this system. Read-only.
SYS-OS.other.act.2=Read the other findings with this in mind, and check how long your version is supported on the website of your distribution.
SYS-OS.ok.title={name}

# ---- SYS-EOL ----
SYS-EOL.ended.title=Release {codename} is no longer supported (standard support ended)
SYS-EOL.ended.impact=Ubuntu no longer publishes security fixes for this release, unless Ubuntu Pro is attached. Security holes found from now on stay open on this computer.
SYS-EOL.ended.act.1=Shows whether Ubuntu Pro is attached. Ubuntu Pro extends security fixes for older releases (free for personal use on up to 5 machines). Read-only.
SYS-EOL.ended.act.2=Checks whether an upgrade to a newer release is offered for this computer. Changes nothing.
SYS-EOL.ended.caution=A release upgrade takes time and cannot be undone. Back up your personal files first, keep a laptop plugged in, and clean up the obsolete packages and third-party repositories listed in this report: they are the usual cause of failed upgrades.
SYS-EOL.soon.title=Standard support for {codename} ends in {days} days
SYS-EOL.soon.impact=After {date}, this release no longer receives security fixes. Your programs keep working, but security holes found after that date stay open.
SYS-EOL.soon.act.1=Checks whether an upgrade to a newer release is offered for this computer. Changes nothing.
SYS-EOL.soon.act.2=Plan the upgrade before that date, at a time when you can do without the computer for an hour or two.
SYS-EOL.soon.caution=A release upgrade cannot be undone. Back up your personal files first, and clean up the obsolete packages and third-party repositories listed in this report: they are the usual cause of failed upgrades.
SYS-EOL.ok.title=Standard support for {codename} until {date}
SYS-EOL.partial.1=support end date not checked (ubuntu-distro-info unavailable)

# ---- SYS-RELEASE ----
SYS-RELEASE.info.title=A newer LTS release is available ({release})
SYS-RELEASE.info.impact=Nothing urgent while your current release is supported. Moving to the newer LTS brings newer software and a later end of support.
SYS-RELEASE.info.act.1=Before any upgrade, clean up the obsolete packages and third-party repositories listed in this report: they are the usual cause of failed upgrades.
SYS-RELEASE.info.act.2=Shows whether the upgrade is already offered for this computer (Ubuntu offers it some time after the release). Changes nothing.

# ---- SYS-REBOOT ----
SYS-REBOOT.warn.title=A reboot is required to finish applying updates
SYS-REBOOT.warn.impact=Some updates are installed but not in use yet, often security fixes for the kernel or core libraries. Until the computer restarts, it stays exposed to the problems they fix.
SYS-REBOOT.warn.act.1=Save your work, then restart the computer from the system menu when it suits you.
SYS-REBOOT.warn.caution=Close your documents before restarting. On a computer that others use remotely, warn them first: its services stop for a few minutes.
SYS-REBOOT.ev.1=Packages requesting the reboot (/var/run/reboot-required.pkgs)
SYS-REBOOT.ok.title=No reboot pending

# ---- SYS-TIME ----
SYS-TIME.warn.title=Clock is not synchronised with NTP (time zone {tz})
SYS-TIME.warn.impact=A clock that drifts makes secure websites and updates fail with certificate errors, and gives wrong times in the logs.
SYS-TIME.warn.act.1=Shows whether automatic time synchronisation is working and which time server it uses. Read-only.
SYS-TIME.warn.act.2=Turns on automatic time synchronisation. Can be undone: the same command with false instead of true turns it off.
SYS-TIME.warn.caution=Just after start-up, or without a network connection, synchronisation can take a few minutes: run the audit again before changing anything.
SYS-TIME.ev.1=timedatectl
SYS-TIME.ok.title=Clock synchronised (time zone {tz})
SYS-TIME.partial.1=clock synchronisation not checked (timedatectl unavailable or no systemd)

# ---- SYS-DISK ----
SYS-DISK.crit.title=Filesystem {target} is {pcent} full
SYS-DISK.crit.impact=When {target} is full, updates stop half-way, programs can no longer save their data, and the system can become unstable. Free some space soon.
SYS-DISK.warn.title=Filesystem {target} is {pcent} full
SYS-DISK.warn.impact=If {target} keeps filling up, updates and programs will start to fail. Freeing space now is easier than when it is full.
SYS-DISK.full.act.1=Lists the packages that are no longer needed (often old kernels) and that apt would remove. Simulation only: nothing is removed.
SYS-DISK.full.act.2=Deletes the downloaded package files kept by apt. Safe: apt downloads them again if it needs them.
SYS-DISK.full.act.3=Shows how much space the system logs use. Read-only.
SYS-DISK.full.act.4=Shows how much space Docker images, containers and volumes use. Read-only.
SYS-DISK.full.act.same=See the actions proposed for {target}: they free space here too.
SYS-DISK.full.caution=Read the list shown by the simulation before running 'sudo apt autoremove' for real, and stop if it contains something you use. A removed package can be installed again; a deleted personal file cannot be recovered.
SYS-DISK.ok.title=Disk usage below {warn}% on system filesystems
SYS-DISK.ev.1=Usage of system filesystems
SYS-DISK.partial.1=disk usage could not be read (df failed)

# ---- SYS-DISK-USAGE ----
SYS-DISK-USAGE.info.title=What takes space and can be freed (largest first)
SYS-DISK-USAGE.info.impact=A filesystem is above {warn}% (see SYS-DISK). The usual places that fill a disk were measured on this computer and are listed below from the largest. The commands for each place of at least 100 MiB are in the actions of SYS-DISK, above in this section: start with the largest places, they free the most.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.none.impact=A filesystem is above {warn}% (see SYS-DISK), but none of the usual places that fill a disk takes 100 MiB or more here. The space is used by something else, often personal files (videos, downloads, virtual machines): the command proposed under SYS-DISK shows the largest folders.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.info.act.other=Lists the folders of {target} from the smallest to the largest, to find where the rest of the space goes. Read-only; it can take a minute.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.info.caution=Every size is measured for the whole computer: some places may be on another filesystem than the full one. Delete nothing you do not recognise: a removed package can be installed again, a deleted personal file cannot be recovered.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.ev.1=Measured size of each place
SYS-DISK-USAGE.size.mib={n} MiB
SYS-DISK-USAGE.size.gib={int}.{dec} GiB
SYS-DISK-USAGE.item.snaprev=old snap versions kept as backups ({n} disabled revision(s))
SYS-DISK-USAGE.item.home=snap data in the user's home folder (~/snap)
SYS-DISK-USAGE.item.docker=Docker data that nothing uses (reclaimable)
SYS-DISK-USAGE.item.kernel=older kernels, besides the running one and the two newest ({kernels} version(s))
SYS-DISK-USAGE.item.journal=system logs (journal)
SYS-DISK-USAGE.item.apt=downloaded package files kept by apt
SYS-DISK-USAGE.snaprev.act.1=Old snap versions take {size} ({n} revision(s)). This lists every snap version: the old ones are marked disabled in the last column. Read-only.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.snaprev.act.2=Deletes one old version of a snap, given its name and revision number (both in the list). Cannot be undone: that version can no longer be restored with 'snap revert'. The version in use is not affected.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.snaprev.act.3=From now on, keeps 2 versions of each snap instead of 3. Can be undone: the same command with 3 goes back to the default.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.home.act.1=The snap data in your home folder takes {size}. This shows the size of each snap's folder, from the smallest to the largest. Read-only.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.home.act.2=A folder of a snap you have removed can be deleted. For a snap you use, free space from within the application (for example the cache and history of the web browser), not by deleting its files: they hold its settings and your data.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.docker.act.1=Docker data that nothing uses takes up to {size}. This lists images, containers and volumes one by one, with their size. Read-only.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.docker.act.2=Deletes stopped containers, unused networks, images without a name and the build cache, after asking for confirmation. Cannot be undone, but images can be downloaded again. Volumes, which hold data, are kept.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.kernel.act.1=Older kernels take {size} ({kernels} version(s)). This lists what apt would remove, kernels included. Simulation only: nothing is removed.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.kernel.act.2=Removes those packages and their settings. apt always keeps the running kernel and the two newest, which are not counted here. A removed kernel can be installed again.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.journal.act.1=The system logs take {size}. This shows the same figure. Read-only.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.journal.act.2=Deletes the oldest logs until 100 MiB are left, which frees about {gain}. Cannot be undone: the deleted logs are lost, recent ones are kept.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.apt.act.1=Downloaded package files take {size}. This shows the same figure. Read-only.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.apt.act.2=Deletes those files. Safe: apt downloads them again if it needs them.
SYS-DISK-USAGE.partial.snaprev=size of old snap versions not measured (snap list --all failed)
SYS-DISK-USAGE.partial.home=size of ~/snap not measured
SYS-DISK-USAGE.partial.docker=Docker data not measured (docker system df failed: Docker not running, or no access without sudo)
SYS-DISK-USAGE.partial.journal=size of the system logs not measured (needs sudo, or membership of the adm group)
SYS-DISK-USAGE.partial.apt=size of the apt package cache not measured

# ---- SYS-LOCAL-BIN ----
SYS-LOCAL-BIN.info.title={n} program(s) in /usr/local/bin or /usr/local/sbin that no package provides
SYS-LOCAL-BIN.info.impact=These programs were installed by hand or by an installer script, outside the package manager: no update ever reaches them, security fixes included. This is an inventory to review, not a fault: some were installed on purpose and are kept up to date in another way.
SYS-LOCAL-BIN.info.act.1=Lists the files of both folders with their size, date and link target. Read-only.
SYS-LOCAL-BIN.info.act.2=For each program you do not recognise, find out where it comes from (its name, the date, the documentation of the software you installed that day) and whether a newer version exists. Remove only what you know: some packages install helper scripts there from their own scripts.
SYS-LOCAL-BIN.ev.1=Programs without a package (size in bytes, date of last change)
SYS-LOCAL-BIN.ev.file={path}: {size} bytes, {date}
SYS-LOCAL-BIN.ev.link={path}: link to {target}, {date}
SYS-LOCAL-BIN.partial.1=programs of /usr/local not compared with the packages (dpkg is not available)
